{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Blast-radius check on root cause B — narrower than feared, stated explicitly\n\nRoot cause B is `forwarded_allow_ips` defaulting to `127.0.0.1` in a container, which makes\nuvicorn distrust EVERY forwarded header from nginx, not just the proto. That is broader than a\nredirect bug in principle, so I checked the two places it would plausibly hurt most rather than\nassuming the redirect was the whole story. Both came back clean, and the honest answer is that\nthe reachable impact is bounded:\n\n**1. RFC 9728 metadata and the `WWW-Authenticate` `resource_metadata` URL — NOT affected.**\nThese are built from the configured `auth_resource_server_url`\n(`src/rebar/mcp_server.py:610`, fed by `REBAR_MCP_AUTH_RESOURCE_SERVER_URL` in\n`docker-compose.yml:347`), not from the request scheme. Confirmed empirically against the live\nbox: the unauthenticated 401 returns\n`resource_metadata=\"https://rebar.solutions.navateam.com/.well-known/oauth-protected-resource/mcp\"`\n— `https`, despite `scope[\"scheme\"]` being `http` at that moment. Config-derived absolute URLs\nare structurally immune to this defect.\n\n**2. Client-IP-keyed logic — none exists in rebar.** A search of `src/rebar/` for\n`request.client`, `scope[\"client\"]`, `remote_addr` and `X-Forwarded-For` consumers returns no\nmatches, so no rebar logging, rate limiting, or audit path keys on the peer address and none is\nsilently recording the docker bridge gateway. (uvicorn's own access log does show the gateway,\nbut that is uvicorn's log line, not rebar logic, and it is corrected by the same\n`FORWARDED_ALLOW_IPS` fix.)\n\n**Conclusion.** The only reachable instance of the defect is a scheme-derived absolute URL, and\nthe slash-redirect `Location` was the one such URL the app actually emits today. So the reported\nbug WAS the blast radius — this is not a redirect symptom of a larger live exposure.\n\n**The residual risk is forward-looking, not current:** any future code that reads\n`request.url`, `request.client`, or builds a URL from the request rather than from config would\nsilently inherit the plaintext scheme. Fixing B here (rather than only patching the edge) is what\nremoves that trap, which is the main reason it is in scope alongside the nginx change.\n\n## Step 7 parity sweep\n\n**Axis 1 (construct sweep): no siblings.** The construct is \"external path with a trailing slash\nproxied URI-preserving to an app mounted at the slash-less path\". `/review/` uses a\nprefix-stripping `proxy_pass .../;` and `/opcert/` preserves a prefix the app genuinely serves,\nso neither has the asymmetry. No other instance in the tree.\n\n**Axis 2 (parity census): three MISSING slots, dispositioned by ticket.** The rule this fix\nenforces — an https request must never receive a Location that downgrades to `http` — now holds\nfor the MCP paths only. `/review/` (`:92`), `/opcert/` (`:107`) and Gerrit's `location /`\n(`:152`) have no equivalent guarantee. I deliberately did NOT hoist `proxy_redirect` to the\nserver level to cover them: it defaults to `default`, derived from each location's own\n`proxy_pass`, so a server-level rule would replace `/review/`'s prefix-stripping rewrite and\ncould break its relative redirects. Filed as\n**a61d-adf9-3725-4847 (enchanting-phantastic-weevil)**, linked `discovered_from` this bug.\n\nHSTS is NOT part of that gap — it is added at the 443 **server** level here, so all four\nsurfaces inherit it immediately.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1787862259377019919,"uuid":"571537e8-ebd8-47fe-961a-95f700c80136"}